{"id":"h168","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A cylinder with a movable piston contains a gas at pressure P = 1 × 10^5 Pa, volume V = 20 cm^3, and temperature T = 273 K. The piston is moved downward in a slow steady fashion allowing heat to escape the gas and the temperature to remain constant. If the final volume of the gas is 5 cm^3, what will be the resulting pressure?\nIs the correct answer \"0.25 × 10^5 Pa\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct final pressure is 4 × 10^5 Pa, not 0.25 × 10^5 Pa.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The process is isothermal, so Boyle's law (PV = constant) applies.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The gas can be treated as ideal.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Compressing the gas from 20 cm³ to 5 cm³ raises the pressure by the volume ratio of 4.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"P2 = P1V1/V2 = 4 × 10^5 Pa.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The proposed answer 0.25 × 10^5 Pa inverts the volume ratio.","confidence":0.92,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"A pressure drop upon compression into a smaller volume is not physically plausible.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Nothing really supports the proposed answer.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":1.0,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":0.9,"r